1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,370
It's eleven forty-eight on a Thursday night.

2
00:00:02,940 --> 00:00:09,660
Your friend Marcus is on your couch, phone in both hands, the cracked case he never got round to replacing.

3
00:00:10,330 --> 00:00:14,330
His trading app says one hundred and eighty-four thousand dollars.

4
00:00:14,720 --> 00:00:17,410
He taps withdraw. A message appears.

5
00:00:17,500 --> 00:00:22,230
Before we can release your funds, please pay a fifteen percent tax.

6
00:00:22,660 --> 00:00:25,089
Twenty-seven thousand six hundred dollars.

7
00:00:25,500 --> 00:00:27,730
He's already put in everything he has.

8
00:00:28,089 --> 00:00:33,219
He's about to pay it. You're the only person in the room who knows what happens next.

9
00:00:33,679 --> 00:00:36,700
Because ninety days ago, you got the same text.

10
00:00:37,450 --> 00:00:39,990
This is a Money Mechanics Friday special.

11
00:00:40,409 --> 00:00:41,750
The story is made up.

12
00:00:42,070 --> 00:00:43,380
The playbook is real.

13
00:00:43,490 --> 00:00:44,960
It's called pig butchering.

14
00:00:45,429 --> 00:00:47,899
The name comes from the scammers themselves.

15
00:00:48,289 --> 00:00:49,969
First, you fatten the pig.

16
00:00:50,380 --> 00:00:51,780
Then, you take everything.

17
00:00:52,240 --> 00:00:54,600
Here's how it works, and how you saw it coming.

18
00:00:55,439 --> 00:00:58,539
Day one. Ten past seven on a Tuesday morning.

19
00:00:58,810 --> 00:01:00,369
A text from a number you don't know.

20
00:01:00,820 --> 00:01:04,459
Hi Anna, are we still on for golf on Saturday?

21
00:01:04,790 --> 00:01:07,219
You're not Anna. Marcus, two desks over, gets one too.

22
00:01:07,640 --> 00:01:09,319
Different name, same message.

23
00:01:09,659 --> 00:01:11,810
He replies, sorry, wrong number.

24
00:01:11,969 --> 00:01:13,290
You don't reply at all.

25
00:01:13,379 --> 00:01:16,590
You don't delete it either. The wrong number isn't a mistake.

26
00:01:16,909 --> 00:01:21,319
It's a filter. Anyone polite enough to answer has just become a lead.

27
00:01:22,090 --> 00:01:25,480
Day twelve. Marcus shows you his phone at lunch.

28
00:01:25,739 --> 00:01:27,019
Her name is Grace.

29
00:01:27,430 --> 00:01:31,000
She texts him good morning at six fifteen, every single day.

30
00:01:31,480 --> 00:01:34,579
She remembers his sister's name, and the job he hates.

31
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:36,500
She's never asked for money.

32
00:01:36,790 --> 00:01:42,480
Not once. Then, almost by accident, she mentions that her uncle taught her to trade crypto.

33
00:01:42,780 --> 00:01:45,500
And she asks to move the chat to another app.

34
00:01:45,920 --> 00:01:48,659
You notice that. Fewer people watching.

35
00:01:48,920 --> 00:01:52,680
Here's the mechanism. The scammer doesn't sell an investment.

36
00:01:52,959 --> 00:01:56,019
They sell a person. The trust comes first.

37
00:01:56,409 --> 00:01:57,670
The money comes later.

38
00:01:58,500 --> 00:02:01,510
Day thirty. Grace sends Marcus a link to an app.

39
00:02:01,739 --> 00:02:05,310
It looks real. Candle charts, a support chat, a logo.

40
00:02:05,480 --> 00:02:07,150
He puts in five hundred dollars.

41
00:02:07,489 --> 00:02:09,860
By Friday, it says six hundred and forty.

42
00:02:10,219 --> 00:02:13,639
He withdraws a hundred, just to test it, and it lands in his bank account.

43
00:02:13,960 --> 00:02:15,639
He's convinced. You're not.

44
00:02:16,080 --> 00:02:18,020
Because that hundred dollars is the bait.

45
00:02:18,590 --> 00:02:19,990
The app is a stage set.

46
00:02:20,530 --> 00:02:23,240
The numbers on the screen are whatever the scammers type.

47
00:02:23,770 --> 00:02:26,840
Letting him take out a little is how they buy the big deposit.

48
00:02:27,669 --> 00:02:30,310
Day sixty. Marcus moves his savings.

49
00:02:30,379 --> 00:02:32,319
Then his retirement account.

50
00:02:32,780 --> 00:02:35,919
Then a loan against the Corolla he'd only just paid off.

51
00:02:36,319 --> 00:02:38,610
One hundred and twenty thousand dollars in.

52
00:02:38,930 --> 00:02:40,370
He stops coming to lunch.

53
00:02:40,759 --> 00:02:43,569
For three weeks, you eat across from his empty chair.

54
00:02:43,919 --> 00:02:46,349
You ask him why. He says he's busy.

55
00:02:46,819 --> 00:02:48,870
That silence is part of the design.

56
00:02:49,310 --> 00:02:52,150
The scam works best when nobody else is looking.

57
00:02:53,009 --> 00:02:56,009
Which brings us back to Thursday night.

58
00:02:56,300 --> 00:03:02,460
And the tax. You pick up your phone, scroll back ninety days, and show him a text you never deleted.

59
00:03:02,610 --> 00:03:05,009
Hi Anna, are we still on for golf on Saturday?

60
00:03:05,389 --> 00:03:08,949
Same opener. Different name.

61
00:03:09,300 --> 00:03:11,060
Then you say the one sentence that matters.

62
00:03:11,210 --> 00:03:15,789
A real exchange doesn't make you send money to get your own money out.

63
00:03:16,340 --> 00:03:18,590
Marcus stops. He doesn't pay.

64
00:03:18,930 --> 00:03:21,439
Grace stops replying within the hour.

65
00:03:21,530 --> 00:03:24,400
Two days later, the app's website disappears.

66
00:03:24,939 --> 00:03:27,240
The hundred and twenty thousand is gone.

67
00:03:27,870 --> 00:03:30,020
But the twenty-seven thousand six hundred isn't.

68
00:03:30,099 --> 00:03:34,500
Most people don't have someone on the couch.

69
00:03:34,580 --> 00:03:43,150
In twenty twenty-four, the FBI counted five point eight billion dollars lost to crypto investment fraud in America, much of it in schemes exactly like this.

70
00:03:43,300 --> 00:03:49,039
People over sixty reported more than two point eight billion dollars lost to crypto-related scams.

71
00:03:49,610 --> 00:03:51,159
And Grace?

72
00:03:51,689 --> 00:04:04,879
Grace might not exist. United Nations investigators estimated at least one hundred and twenty thousand people in Myanmar, and around one hundred thousand in Cambodia, may be held in compounds and forced to run online scams.

73
00:04:05,009 --> 00:04:08,759
Some of the people typing good morning are prisoners too.

74
00:04:09,629 --> 00:04:11,340
So, how do you jam the machine?

75
00:04:11,699 --> 00:04:18,370
Rule one. A stranger who drifts from small talk to investing is a red flag, every single time.

76
00:04:18,790 --> 00:04:23,019
Rule two. If you have to pay to withdraw, the money was never there.

77
00:04:23,620 --> 00:04:28,029
Rule three. Before you send anything, tell one person you trust.

78
00:04:28,329 --> 00:04:30,910
Or better, be that person for someone else.

79
00:04:31,670 --> 00:04:33,629
Pig butchering doesn't hack your phone.

80
00:04:34,069 --> 00:04:35,990
It hacks loneliness, and hope.

81
00:04:36,459 --> 00:04:39,110
The best defence is someone who knows the playbook.

82
00:04:39,569 --> 00:04:46,810
Now, that's you. Next time on Money Mechanics, how airlines really make money, and why it's not the flights.

